exercises.solution:
```python
def cosets(n, k):
    H = sorted({k * m % n for m in range(n)})
    result = []
    for a in range(n):
        c = sorted({(a + h) % n for h in H})
        if c not in result:
            result.append(c)
    return sorted(result)

def coset_sum(n, k, a, b):
    H = sorted({k * m % n for m in range(n)})
    return sorted({(a + b + h) % n for h in H})
```

The last test checks well-definedness by brute force: every choice of representatives of two cosets gives the same coset of the sum. In an abelian group every subgroup is normal, so this always succeeds; the analogous test for $\{e, (1\,2)\}$ in $S_3$ fails (see the first experiment). Note that $\langle 4 \rangle = \langle \gcd(4, 10) \rangle = \langle 2 \rangle$ in $\Z/10\Z$.
